{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Take the staff, and speak to the rock.</b> Opinions already abound concerning the sin of the waters of Meribah, and many were uncertain as to what Moses and Aaron sinned, for it was written of them: לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם, מְעַלְתֶּם (Deuteronomy 32:51), and מְרִיתֶם (Numbers 20:24). And if the intent of the blessed El was that they should merely speak to the rock, what is the relevance of taking the staff? And if the sin was that Moses struck the rock without the command of Him who sent him, how did Aaron sin? Now, when we consider the matter of Meribah, it is fitting to suppose that the command of the El in this matter was ordered so that Israel would recognize the wickedness of their quarrel, confess their sin, repent, and He would heal them — for He does not desire the death of the dying. And so we understand that in this matter Moses and Aaron frustrated the intent of the blessed El, and for this He punished them. Now, the quarrel with Moses was in their saying that his conduct was imperfect, in that he brought them to that evil part of the wilderness; and their quarrel was against the blessed El, in their saying that He brought them out of a good inhabited land into a desert land. And to make known to them their wickedness in this, it was necessary that the miracle demonstrate that the emissary had conducted himself with perfection, and that the Sender had done good through His mission and had done no evil at all. And since the kinds of miracles recounted in sacred writings are of three types: the first is a hidden miracle, such as the descent of rain and deliverance from diseases and troubles, and this kind of miracle the righteous attain through their prayer — as in וַיִּתְפַּלֵּל אַבְרָהָם אֶל הָאֱלֹהִים וַיִּרְפָּא אֱלֹהִים אֶת אֲבִימֶלֶךְ (Genesis 20:17), and likewise וַיִּתְפַּלֵּל מֹשֶׁה בְּעַד הָעָם (Numbers 21:7). The second is a revealed miracle, which nature cannot produce in that manner, but which it can produce after many movements over a span of time. And this kind of miracle the blessed El performs through His servants with the prior arrangement of an ordered motion from Him — as in הַשְׁלִיכֵהוּ אַרְצָה (Exodus 4:3), הָרֵם אֶת מַטְּךָ וְהַכֵּיתָ בַצּוּר (Exodus 17:6), יְרֵה וַיּוֹר (II Kings 13:17), and others. And the third is a kind of miracle which nature cannot produce in any manner whatsoever, and this kind the exalted El performs through His servants by speech alone, which is an intellectual action and more distinguished than His other corporeal motions — as was the case with the opening of the earth, as it says: וַיְהִי כְּכַלֹּתוֹ לְדַבֵּר וַתִּבָּקַע הָאֲדָמָה (Numbers 16:31), and likewise concerning the sun's standing for Joshua it says אָז יְדַבֵּר יְהוֹשֻׁעַ (Joshua 10:12). Now, in the matter of this Meribah it was necessary, in order to remove the evil of Israel's complaints, that the miracle performed make known the excellence of the sending King and the ways of His goodness, and also make known the excellence and goodness of the emissary, and that he is prepared for the King to do good through him for His people. And the excellence of the Sender would be made known through the third kind of miracle, over which nature has no power in any manner or at any time, and the excellence of the emissary would be made known through the second kind of miracle, performed through the motion of the emissary suited for this. And so the blessed El commanded that the rock itself be transformed into water, as it says וְנָתַן מֵימָיו — that is, that the waters should come from the rock, not drawn to it from elsewhere. And this would have been impossible by any other means than its being transformed from the form of rock to the form of water, as He Himself testified afterward, saying: הַמּוֹצִיא לְךָ מַיִם מִצּוּר הַחַלָּמִישׁ (Deuteronomy 8:15) — for if the waters had been brought from elsewhere by miracle, there would be no difference between flint rock and anything else. And this kind of miracle nature cannot perform in any manner or at any time, and He commanded that this be done through the speech of His servants, as it says וְדִבַּרְתֶּם אֶל הַסֶּלַע, in order that Israel recognize that this miracle was of the third kind, as stated — not that waters flowed there from elsewhere. And through this they would know the greatness and goodness of the Sender, and take to heart that even though He brought them out from Egypt to the wilderness, there is no evil in this at all so long as He is with them, since it is in His power to make a wilderness into a pool of water — and this in a way that surpasses nature — and that if He is here, all is here, as it says הַמִּדְבָּר הָיִיתִי לְיִשְׂרָאֵל (Jeremiah 2:31). And together with this He commanded that after the rock was transformed into water, Moses should bring forth that water to them with his staff to their tribes, as it says בִּמְחֹקֵק בְּמִשְׁעֲנֹתָם (Numbers 21:18). And concerning this kind of miracle He said: Take the staff and bring forth for them water from the rock, etc. Now, Moses and Aaron agreed to perform the second kind of miracle, and to channel the water to the rock from elsewhere, as they did at Rephidim, as it says וְהִכֵּיתָ בַצּוּר וְיָצְאוּ מִמֶּנּוּ מַיִם (Exodus 17:6) — for they did not trust that the blessed El would fulfill His word to perform for Israel the third kind of miracle, reckoning that Israel at that time were exceedingly rebellious, so as to be unworthy of it, and that HaShem would relent from the good. And so they performed the way of the second kind of miracle with the staff, through which the excellence of the emissary is made known, and did not perform the way of the third kind, through which they would make known to Israel the excellence of the Sender and His goodness. And therefore it was written of them: לֹא הֶאֱמַנְתֶּם בִּי — you did not trust in My will, that I would do what I said; וּמְעַלְתֶּם בִּי (Deuteronomy 32:51) — you profaned My honor and did not show the quarrelers their folly; וּמְרִיתֶם פִּי — in that you did not keep My commandment."], :book :numbers, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:40:41.664131802Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}